(* ============================================================================
   PART 6: INCLUDES
   ============================================================================ *)

(* PROCESSOR-LEVEL (PART 9 §19): includes are NOT part of the core parser
   and the directive is deliberately NOT reachable from `block`/`inline` --
   a conformant core may leave `{{ … }}` literal. *)
include_directive = "{{", whitespace+, include_path, [include_section], [include_options], whitespace+, "}}" ;
(* The padding is a RUN, not one space. One whitespace character is required on
   each side - `{{c.crv}}` and `{{ c.crv}}` are literal text - because that is
   what keeps a path from starting at `{`. Beyond the first, more changes
   nothing: a bare path already stops at the first space and a quoted path
   carries its own delimiters. Refusing the extra would turn an aligned
   directive into ordinary prose with NO warning, which is the one failure mode
   section 19's error rules exist to avoid. Tabs count, as they do everywhere
   else whitespace does. *)
(* THE CLOSER is the first "}}" that falls OUTSIDE any quoted run. A quoted run
   -- `quoted_include_path` here, or the `quoted_value` form of an option's
   `attribute_value` (PART 4) -- MAY contain the "}}" pair: both productions
   exclude only their own quote character, the backslash and the newline, so a
   "}}" between the quotes belongs to the quoted run and does NOT end the
   directive. Both spellings therefore close at the "}}" that FOLLOWS the
   closing quote, and what comes after it is ordinary text:

     {{ "a }} more" @k:v }} end
     {{ ch.crv @label:"a }} more" }} end

   A recognizer that scans for the first "}}" without tracking quoting ends the
   first of those at its PATH's pair and the second at its VALUE's, which is the
   divergence this clause settles: the two were measured to disagree, and the
   grammar as written was always on the side of admitting the pair.
   An UNTERMINATED quote does not open a run. It falls back to the unquoted
   reading, so it can never pair with a quote further along the line, and the
   closer is again the first "}}" - which is what leaves a malformed directive
   as the literal text section 19 says it is. *)
include_path = bare_include_path | quoted_include_path ;
bare_include_path = {character - ('#' | '@' | '}' | ' ' | newline)}+ ;
(* A path carrying a space is DOUBLE-QUOTED, and only then: the bare form stops
   at the first space, so an unquoted `my chapter.crv` ends the path at `my`
   and the rest is read as the option slot it is not. `\"` is a literal quote
   inside the quoted form. *)
quoted_include_path = '"', {('\\', '"') | (character - ('"' | newline))}+, '"' ;
include_section = '#', identifier ;
include_options = {whitespace+, '@', identifier, ':', attribute_value}+ ;

